sec.p:
To reconstruct the participant’s estimated path from the angular response, we treated the reported heading angle θt (radians) as specifying the instantaneous lateral slope of the perceived trajectory at frame t. For each time step, we computed the incremental simulated forward displacement
(3)
Δzt=zt−zt−1, \begin{document}$$\displaystyle \begin{equation*} \Delta z_{t}=z_{t}-z_{t-1}, \tag{3} \end{equation*}$$\end{document}
and converted the response angle into a lateral increment via
(4)
Δxt=tan⁡θtΔzt. \begin{document}$$\displaystyle \begin{equation*} \Delta x_{t}=\tan \theta_{t} \Delta z_{t} . \tag{4} \end{equation*}$$\end{document}
The estimated lateral position was then obtained by forward integration starting from the initial position x0:
(5)
xt=x0+∑k=1ttan⁡θkΔzk. \begin{document}$$\displaystyle \begin{equation*} x_{t}=x_{0}+\sum_{k=1}^{t} \tan \theta_{k} \Delta z_{k} . \tag{5} \end{equation*}$$\end{document}
This yields the sample-wise reconstructed lateral trajectory xt consistent with the participant’s angular responses.

We interpret the reported path as the output of a curl–driven feedback mechanism inspired by point attractor models used previously in steering control (Fajen and Warren, 2007; Wilkie and Wann, 2003). Let θt denote the heading direction (direction of forward velocity in world coordinates) and ψt the gaze direction. We consider their difference
ϕt=ψt−θt \begin{document}$$\displaystyle \phi_{t}=\psi_{t}-\theta_{t}$$\end{document}
as a instantaneous heading error. The mean optic–flow curl ωt\begin{document}$\omega_{t}$\end{document} is taken as a sensory measurement of this discrepancy, so that ϕt≈ωt/kc\begin{document}$\phi_{t} \approx \omega_{t} / k_{c}$\end{document}, with kc being a curl–to–angle scale factor. This is motivated by the fact that curl increases whenever there is a discrepancy between θ and ψ. The observer controls heading by applying a yaw rate input ωt=θ˙t\begin{document}$\omega_{t}=\dot{\theta}_{t}$\end{document}:
(6)
θ˙t=ωt=Kpϕt≈Kpωtkc \begin{document}$$\displaystyle \begin{equation*} \dot{\theta}_{t}=\omega_{t}=K_{p} \phi_{t} \approx K_{p} \frac{\omega_{t}}{k_{c}} \tag{6} \end{equation*}$$\end{document}
so that positive curl induces a leftward turn (increasing θ) and negative curl induces a rightward turn. If gaze is held fixed (e.g. steady fixation), ψ˙t=0\begin{document}$\dot{\psi}_{t}=0$\end{document} and therefore
(7)
ϕt=−θt=−Kpϕt \begin{document}$$\displaystyle \begin{equation*} \phi_{t}=-\theta_{t}=-K_{p} \phi_{t} \tag{7} \end{equation*}$$\end{document}
This is a simple first-order linear differential equation, and the heading error decays exponentially with a correction time constant 1/Kp:
(8)
ϕt=ϕ0e−Kpt \begin{document}$$\displaystyle \begin{equation*} \phi_{t}=\phi_{0} e^{-K_{p} t} \tag{8} \end{equation*}$$\end{document}
Thus, heading is continuously steered toward gaze until curl vanishes. In our reconstruction we integrate this controlled heading forward in time to generate the perceived trajectory implied by the observed curl (see video S3 in the SI).

To test if perceived trajectories can be accounted for by the curl signal, we used the controller-defined in Equation 6 which we integrated forward with an explicit Euler step to obtain the estimated heading θ. With sampling interval Δt, constant forward speed (v), and initial state (θ0, x0, z0) taken from the first sample of each trial, the discrete update is
(9)
θt+1=θt+KpKcω‾tΔtzt+1=zt+vcos⁡θtΔtxt+1=xt+vsin⁡θtΔt \begin{document}$$\displaystyle \begin{array}{cc} \theta_{t+1} = \theta_t + \frac{K_p}{K_c} \bar{\omega}_t \Delta t \\ \\ z_{t+1}=z_{t}+v \cos \theta_{t} \Delta t \quad x_{t+1}=x_{t}+v \sin \theta_{t} \Delta t \end{array} $$\end{document}
This generates a predicted 2D path Ppred(Kp)={(zt,xt)}t=1T\begin{document}$P_{\text {pred }}\left(K_{p}\right)=\left\{\left(z_{t}, x_{t}\right)\right\}_{t=1}^{T}$\end{document} from the observed mean curl (ω)\begin{document}$(\omega)$\end{document} sequence. We then estimate Kp and forward velocity (v) as free parameters by minimizing a time-warped path discrepancy (J) using Dynamic Time Warping (DTW)(Giorgino, 2009) on the (z, x) sequences between the predicted and perceived paths:
J(Kp)=DTW(Pobs,Ppred(Kp)) \begin{document}$$\displaystyle J\left(K_{p}\right)=\operatorname{DTW}\left(P_{\mathrm{obs}}, P_{\mathrm{pred}}\left(K_{p}\right)\right)$$\end{document}
where
Pobs={(ztobs,xtobs)}t=1T \begin{document}$$\displaystyle P_{\mathrm{obs}}=\left\{\left(z_{t}^{\mathrm{obs}}, x_{t}^{\mathrm{obs}}\right)\right\}_{t=1}^{T}$$\end{document}
is the observed path.

In order to compare the different models, we employed information criteria adapted for distance-based model comparison. For each model, we computed a surrogate log-likelihood based on the normalized alignment cost:
log⁡L=−12(Dn) \begin{document}$$\displaystyle \log \mathcal{L}=-\frac{1}{2}\left(\frac{D}{n}\right)$$\end{document}
where D represents the total DTW distance and n the number of observations, with D/n representing the average alignment cost per observation. This normalization ensures appropriate scaling for information criteria computation. We then computed the Akaike Information Criterion as:
AIC=2k−2log⁡L \begin{document}$$\displaystyle \mathrm{AIC}=2 k-2 \log \mathcal{L}$$\end{document}
where k represents the number of parameters.

Dividing by Z and substituting the image coordinate y=YZ\begin{document}$y=\frac{Y}{Z}$\end{document}, we get the inverse depth profile of the ground:
(10)
1Z=sin⁡α−ycos⁡αh \begin{document}$$\displaystyle \begin{equation*} \frac{1}{Z}=\frac{\sin \alpha-y \cos \alpha}{h} \tag{10} \end{equation*}$$\end{document}
Notice that looking vertically in the image plane changes the depth. The gradient of inverse depth at the fovea (x = 0, y = 0) is:
(11)
∂∂x(1Z)=0 \begin{document}$$\displaystyle \begin{equation*} \frac{\partial}{\partial x}\left(\frac{1}{Z}\right)=0 \tag{11} \end{equation*}$$\end{document}
(12)
∂∂y(1Z)=−cos⁡αh \begin{document}$$\displaystyle \begin{equation*} \frac{\partial}{\partial y}\left(\frac{1}{Z}\right)=-\frac{\cos \alpha}{h} \tag{12} \end{equation*}$$\end{document}

The standard optic flow equations for translation on the image plane (fx, fy) are:
(13)
fx=−vx+xvzZ \begin{document}$$\displaystyle \begin{equation*} f_{x}=\frac{-v_{x}+x v_{z}}{Z} \tag{13} \end{equation*}$$\end{document}
(14)
fy=−vy+yvzZ \begin{document}$$\displaystyle \begin{equation*} f_{y}=\frac{-v_{y}+y v_{z}}{Z} \tag{14} \end{equation*}$$\end{document}
The 2D curl at the fovea is defined as
(15)
C=∂fy∂x−∂fx∂y \begin{document}$$\displaystyle \begin{equation*} C=\frac{\partial f_{y}}{\partial x}-\frac{\partial f_{x}}{\partial y} \tag{15} \end{equation*}$$\end{document}
Evaluating this at the origin (x = 0, y = 0):
(16)
∂fy∂x=−vy∂(1/Z)∂x=0 \begin{document}$$\displaystyle \begin{equation*} \frac{\partial f_{y}}{\partial x}=-v_{y} \frac{\partial(1 / Z)}{\partial x}=0 \tag{16} \end{equation*}$$\end{document}
(17)
∂fx∂y=−vx∂(1/Z)∂y=−vx(−cos⁡αh)=vxcos⁡αh \begin{document}$$\displaystyle \begin{equation*} \frac{\partial f_{x}}{\partial y}=-v_{x} \frac{\partial(1 / Z)}{\partial y}=-v_{x}\left(-\frac{\cos \alpha}{h}\right)=v_{x} \frac{\cos \alpha}{h} \tag{17} \end{equation*}$$\end{document}
Subtracting the two gives the translational curl:
(18)
Ctrans=0−(vxcos⁡αh)=−vxcos⁡αh \begin{document}$$\displaystyle \begin{equation*} C_{t r a n s}=0-\left(v_{x} \frac{\cos \alpha}{h}\right)=-v_{x} \frac{\cos \alpha}{h} \tag{18} \end{equation*}$$\end{document}
Substituting our earlier vx = −v sin ψ into equation Equation 18 we obtain the final expression for curl:
(18, 2)
Ctrans=v⋅sin⁡ψcos⁡αh \begin{document}$$\displaystyle \begin{equation*} C_{\text {trans }}=v \cdot \sin \psi \frac{\cos \alpha}{h} \tag{18,2} \end{equation*}$$\end{document}

To quantify the rotational (curl) component of optic flow around the current gaze position, we project local flow vectors onto the tangential direction of the circle centered at gaze. For each sampled location pi, we first compute its position relative to the gaze point g, also encoded in image coordinates (xi, yi):
(22)
ri=pi−g \begin{document}$$\displaystyle \begin{equation*} \mathbf{r}_{i}=\mathbf{p}_{i}-\mathbf{g} \tag{22} \end{equation*}$$\end{document}
This vector points from the gaze location to the sample point. The direction orthogonal to this vector corresponds to the local tangential direction of rotation around gaze. We obtain this unit tangential vector by rotating ri by 90° and normalizing:
(23)
t^i=[−ri,y,ri,x]T‖ri‖ \begin{document}$$\displaystyle \begin{equation*} \hat{\mathbf{t}}_{i}=\frac{\left[-r_{i, y}, r_{i, x}\right]^{T}}{\left\|\mathbf{r}_{i}\right\|} \tag{23} \end{equation*}$$\end{document}
Finally, the contribution of the optic flow at that point to local rotational motion is given by the projection of the flow vector vi onto this tangential direction:
ωi=vi⋅t^i \begin{document}$$\displaystyle \omega_{i}=\mathbf{v}_{i} \cdot \hat{\mathbf{t}}_{i}$$\end{document}
Positive values indicate counterclockwise motion around gaze, and negative values indicate clockwise motion. The mean curl is computed as follows:
ω¯=1Nused∑i∈Sωi \begin{document}$$\displaystyle \bar{\omega}=\frac{1}{N_{\text {used }}} \sum_{i \in S} \omega_{i}$$\end{document}
where S represents samples satisfying ‖ri‖ > rmin after trimming outliers. In our implementation, Ntotal = 400 samples are drawn within a gaze-centered region.

Heading direction θ is represented by a ring attractor mechanism consistent with previous studies (Zhang, 1996), with neural activity x(ϕ, t) at preferred heading ϕ evolving as:
(24)
τdx(ϕ,t)dt=−x(ϕ,t)+∫W(ϕ,ϕ′)x(ϕ′,t)dϕ′+Iext(ϕ,t) \begin{document}$$\displaystyle \begin{equation*} \tau \frac{d x(\phi, t)}{d t}=-x(\phi, t)+\int W\left(\phi, \phi^{\prime}\right) x\left(\phi^{\prime}, t\right) d \phi^{\prime}+I_{\mathrm{ext}}(\phi, t) \tag{24} \end{equation*}$$\end{document}
We implement these dynamics by using a discrete ring of N = 181 units with preferred headings ϕj uniformly spaced between [−ϕmax, ϕmax]. The activity dynamics then follow:
(25)
xj[t+1]=xj[t]+Δtτ(−xj[t]+∑k=1NWjkxk[t]+Iext,j[t]) \begin{document}$$\displaystyle \begin{equation*} x_{j}[t+1]=x_{j}[t]+\frac{\Delta t}{\tau}\left(-x_{j}[t]+\sum_{k=1}^{N} W_{j k} x_{k}[t]+I_{\mathrm{ext}, j}[t]\right) \tag{25} \end{equation*}$$\end{document}
The recurrent connectivity follows a standard Mexican hat profile (Ben-Yishai et al., 1995):
(26)
Wjk=Aeexp⁡(−d(ϕj,ϕk)22σe2)−Aiexp⁡(−d(ϕj,ϕk)22σi2) \begin{document}$$\displaystyle \begin{equation*} W_{j k}=A_{e} \exp \left(-\frac{d\left(\phi_{j}, \phi_{k}\right)^{2}}{2 \sigma_{e}^{2}}\right)-A_{i} \exp \left(-\frac{d\left(\phi_{j}, \phi_{k}\right)^{2}}{2 \sigma_{i}^{2}}\right) \tag{26} \end{equation*}$$\end{document}
where d(ϕj, ϕk) is the circular distance between preferred headings. Ae and Ai were set to 1.9 and 1.0 respectively.

The neural heading estimate θpx, decoded from the ring attractor’s peak activity, was converted from image coordinates to an ego-centric heading angle θrad using a pinhole camera model. Before converting to angular units, we smoothed the ring attractor’s decoded signal to align with psychophysical evidence that complex motion is integrated over longer time scales than local signals (Burr and Santoro, 2001). We used a temporal window of 2.4 seconds. Given a horizontal field of view (F OVH = 100°) and an image width (W = 800 pixels), the camera’s focal length in pixels was defined as:


f=W2tan⁡(FOVH2)
\begin{document}$$\displaystyle f=\frac{W}{2 \tan \left(\frac{F O V_{H}}{2}\right)}$$\end{document}



The instantaneous heading in radians was then calculated by taking the inverse tangent of the pixel displacement relative to the principal point:


θrad=arctan⁡(θpxf)
\begin{document}$$\displaystyle \theta_{\text {rad }}=\arctan \left(\frac{\theta_{p x}}{f}\right)$$\end{document}



To reconstruct the locomotor path in 3D space, we assumed a constant forward velocity v = 0.7 m/s. Under the assumption of a flat ground plane (fixed height y), the agent’s position in world coordinates (X, Z) was updated via path integration. For each time step Δt = 0.033s, the position transition was defined as:


[Xt+1Zt+1]=[XtZt]+[v⋅sin⁡(θrad)v⋅cos⁡(θrad)]Δt
\begin{document}$$\displaystyle \left[\begin{array}{c}X_{t+1} \\ Z_{t+1}\end{array}\right]=\left[\begin{array}{c}X_{t} \\ Z_{t}\end{array}\right]+\left[\begin{array}{c}v \cdot \sin \left(\theta_{r a d}\right) \\ v \cdot \cos \left(\theta_{r a d}\right)\end{array}\right] \Delta t$$\end{document}



where Z represents the forward depth axis and X the lateral displacement. This way we were able to compare the reproduced path from the network model with the experimental data.
